{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"`bridge fsck` advertises four checks in its docstring. Three are permanently empty and one is\nunreachable; only `binding_drift` — added later by bug `8de5-31b4-aa13-4ed9` — does real work.\n\nRoot cause: the module arrived verbatim from the pre-rebar `digital-service-orchestra` plugin\n(extraction commit `b534f47a7f`), and **SYNC events never existed in rebar**. On `origin/tickets`\ntoday: 39,216 event files, 0 SYNC, 0 BRIDGE_ALERT. `if not sync_events: continue`\n(`bridge_fsck.py:156-158`) short-circuits every ticket dir, killing the orphaned, duplicate and\nstale arms together. The level-triggered cutover (ADRs 0026-0029, epic `3006-e198-13db-4e1f`)\nthen replaced event signalling with state entirely — those four ADRs mention SYNC and BRIDGE_ALERT\nzero times as bridge signals. The \"unresolved BRIDGE_ALERT counts\" line was never backed by code in\nany commit.\n\nWhere the capability actually lives now: orphaned -> `audit_binding_drift`'s `local_gone` cell\n(`bridge_fsck.py:338,365`); duplicates -> `detect_at_most_one_local_id`\n(`rebar_reconciler/invariants.py:121-146`, online only); stale -> the heartbeat canary, and no\noffline analog is wanted.\n\nTwo genuine gaps remain, plus one already ticketed:\n\n- **G1** `count_unresolved_alerts` (`_engine_support/bridge.py:35-74`) is implemented and correct,\n  but its sole caller is gated behind `.bridge-status.json` — a file with **no producer anywhere in\n  the tree or history** — so `rebar bridge-status` always exits 1 before reaching it. Wiring, not\n  new logic. Source events are on the `tickets` branch and durable, unlike the gitignored\n  `bridge_state/alerts/`.\n- **G2** No offline duplicate/at-most-one audit. `bindings.json` carries a `reverse` map that\n  `audit_binding_drift` already reads (`:390,406`) but never validates for forward/reverse\n  consistency. A `store_integrity` cell would be decidable **fully offline with zero Jira access**.\n- **G3** is already tracked as janitor ticket `8efe-9ceb-7d89-4a33` (`BridgeFsckOut` omits\n  `binding_drift`) — two independent investigations reached it separately.\n\nAlso: `bridge.py:6` cites \"the level-triggered reconciler cutover (epic 3a03)\" — no such rebar epic\nor ADR exists. Inherited verbatim from the DSO-era header; unrelated to epic 3006.\n\n## Decisions taken (resolved 2026-08-06; no decision is deferred to implementation)\n\n1. **Removal is BREAKING — the empty keys go.** `orphaned`, `duplicates` and `stale` are removed\n   from the output, not retained as permanently-empty keys. The only in-repo consumer reads\n   `binding_drift` alone (`scripts/canary_bridge.py:419`), so nothing breaks. Retaining empty keys\n   would itself violate this ticket's \"advertises only checks it can actually perform\" criterion.\n   The output contract is already changing under G3 (`8efe-9ceb-7d89-4a33`), so it breaks ONCE,\n   with a CHANGELOG entry.\n\n2. **`unknown_event_types` SURVIVES, but the per-ticket walk does not.** Replace the 21k-file JSON\n   walk with a two-stage derivation:\n   * **Stage 1 (filter)** — one `git grep -h -o -E '\"event_type\"[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*\"[^\"]*\"'`\n     over the tickets ref. Measured on the live store: **0.69s vs 2.67s** for the Python walk, and\n     it needs NO checkout. The `[[:space:]]*` is required — the store contains both\n     `\"event_type\":\"X\"` and `\"event_type\": \"X\"`.\n   * **Stage 2 (confirm)** — subtract `KNOWN_EVENT_TYPES | _NON_REPLAY_KNOWN_TYPES`\n     (`reducer/_version.py:87-94`, pure set membership — no ordering, nothing lost). Empty residual\n     (the normal case) returns immediately having opened zero files. A NON-empty residual opens only\n     the matching files and parses them properly, so a grep false positive can never reach the\n     output.\n   Measured false-positive headroom: the grep yields **21,047 matches against 21,176 parsed files** —\n   fewer matches than files, so no nested `\"event_type\"` occurrences exist in comment bodies or\n   `SNAPSHOT.compiled_state` today. Stage 2 makes that safe permanently rather than by luck.\n   With the walk gone, `audit_bridge_mappings` collapses to a thin wrapper over\n   `audit_binding_drift`.\n\n3. **The BRIDGE_ALERT emitter is DELETED, and G1 with it.** The precondition this ticket asked about\n   (\"is identity-write rollback handled elsewhere?\") is already satisfied, and the emitter's own\n   preamble documents it (`dispatch_one.py:320-332`): the rollback was REMOVED because\n   \"the write-ahead above already recorded the key ... The rollback was solving a problem the\n   write-ahead protocol had already solved, and destroying data to do it.\" The alert therefore fires\n   on a path that no longer executes — corroborated by **0 BRIDGE_ALERT events across the store's\n   entire history**. Wiring `count_unresolved_alerts` through would make a dead path observable\n   instead of removing it. Delete the emitter (`dispatch_one.py:353`), the reducer arm,\n   `_engine_support/bridge.py`, and the docstring line together.\n\n4. **No runbook depends on `.bridge-status.json`.** Verified: zero references across `docs/`,\n   `scripts/` and `.github/`. The claim at `bridge.py:5-7` that its always-exit-1 behaviour is\n   \"the contract operator runbooks rely on\" is FALSE in-tree. Safe to delete.\n\n5. **Ownership: this is new work under `2f79`; `8de5` closes separately.**\n   `8de5-31b4-aa13-4ed9` is `in_progress` under the closed epic `3006-e198-13db-4e1f` and its\n   acceptance criteria are already satisfied by `audit_binding_drift`. It is closed as done rather\n   than left open to absorb this ticket, which stays under the bridge-redesign epic.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `bridge fsck` advertises only checks it can actually perform\n- [ ] The dead stale-detection machinery is gone: `_STALE_THRESHOLD_NS`, `_NS_THRESHOLD`, `_to_ns`,\n      the `--now-ts` flag, and the SYNC-gated arms of the event-scan loop\n- [ ] `unknown_event_types` is derived by the two-stage grep-then-confirm path, and the per-ticket\n      event walk in `audit_bridge_mappings` is deleted\n- [ ] A test plants an event type absent from `KNOWN_EVENT_TYPES` and asserts it is reported\n- [ ] A test asserts BOTH spacing variants are matched (`\"event_type\":\"X\"` and `\"event_type\": \"X\"`)\n- [ ] A test plants `\"event_type\"` inside a comment body / `SNAPSHOT.compiled_state` and asserts it\n      is NOT reported — proving stage 2 suppresses grep false positives\n- [ ] `orphaned`, `duplicates` and `stale` are removed from the output (breaking), with CHANGELOG\n- [ ] The BRIDGE_ALERT emitter, its reducer arm and `_engine_support/bridge.py` are deleted together;\n      no implemented-but-unreachable alert counter remains\n- [ ] G2 `store_integrity` validates forward/reverse binding consistency offline with no Jira access\n- [ ] G1 is DELETED along with its emitter (decision 3) — `count_unresolved_alerts` and its\n      `.bridge-status.json` gate are gone, not left implemented-and-unreachable\n- [ ] Any informational count does NOT gate the exit code (bug `f436`'s failure mode: one\n      never-resolved alert reds the canary forever)\n- [ ] Golden CLI help and CHANGELOG updated for any output-contract change\n- [ ] Tests that synthesize SYNC fixtures purely to exercise dead arms are removed with them\n      (`tests/scripts/bridge/test_bridge_fsck.py`, `tests/scripts/bridge_test_helpers.py:61-63`)\n- [ ] The dangling \"epic 3a03\" citation at `bridge.py:6` is corrected or removed"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786082171816900001,"uuid":"276199b1-d221-426b-a5e5-0e8a459c3a9c"}